.env*
# ...but not the example, which exists to be read by whoever sets the real ones up. `.env*` matched
# it too, so the one file here that is meant to be committed was the one file that never was.
!.env.example

# The booking database. Personal data on one side and a file that differs on every machine on the
# other; in production it lives on the mounted volume. Committing it would publish customers' names
# and phone numbers to the repository.
#
# Anchored with a leading slash, and it has to stay that way. A bare `data/` matches a directory of
# that name at *any* depth, which also swallows `__commitpress__/data/` — the data-source
# definitions. That failure is silent and total: the file stays on the laptop that wrote it, the CMS
# reads sources from the repository and finds none, and the deployed descriptor endpoint answers 500
# because the file it reads was never in the build.
/data/

